# CONTROL Resonant (Remedy's Northlight engine, DirectX 12 via VKD3D-Proton)
# summary: Bigger audio buffer, Proton's Xalia helper off, DualSense haptics over Bluetooth too
# Wine asks PipeWire for so little buffering that the HDMI output runs 128-sample (2.7 ms) cycles, which underran
# under load in Galactic Racer. 90 ms requested latency gives 1024-sample cycles.
PULSE_LATENCY_MSEC=90
# Proton's Xalia (controller navigation for launchers) is not needed: the game has its own controller support, and
# Xalia used a third of a core while the game ran.
PROTON_USE_XALIA=0
# DualSense: the game drives it itself through Sony's libScePad (raw HID, which Proton gives it by default), with
# Wwise sending haptics as audio on channels 3-4 of the pad's USB sound card ("Wireless Controller" in Proton).
# - USB: haptics and triggers, as long as the pad is on USB when the game starts (a pad switched to USB while the
#   game was loading got triggers and no haptics). controller/61-gamespark-dualsense-usb.rules moves a
#   Bluetooth-connected pad to USB when the cable goes in.
# - Bluetooth: triggers only (the game sends no vibration at all) unless DUALSENSE_BT_HAPTICS=1: then
#   controller/dualsense-bt.sh shows the pad to Proton as a USB one with a stand-in sound card and turns its haptics
#   into Bluetooth haptics reports. The pad must be connected when the game starts.
# Game audio stays on the TV; a DualSense sound card is never the default output
# (controller/51-gamespark-dualsense-not-default.lua).
# The shim drops feature reports the game writes: one (0x08, USB setup) made the pad drop its Bluetooth link.
DUALSENSE_BT_HAPTICS=1
# Haptics strength over Bluetooth (1.0 = as the game plays them); the user picked 90%.
DUALSENSE_HAPTICS_GAIN=0.9
